00:07:48.190 --> 00:07:50.610
if they choose to,
or do it all together

00:07:50.610 --> 00:07:53.890
in a hybrid environment,
and choose providers

00:07:53.890 --> 00:07:57.580
that really give you the best
and most innovative services.

00:07:57.580 --> 00:07:59.080
So all this really
brings us down

00:07:59.080 --> 00:08:01.550
to some of the last thoughts
I wanted to leave you with,

00:08:01.550 --> 00:08:03.106
which is our Cloud
trust principles.

00:08:03.106 --> 00:08:04.480
There's about five
of them that I

00:08:04.480 --> 00:08:06.105
want to talk to you
about before I pass

00:08:06.105 --> 00:08:07.840
the mic on to our next speaker.

00:08:07.840 --> 00:08:09.850
So let's begin
with the first one.

00:08:09.850 --> 00:08:14.060
Know that your security comes
first in everything that we do.

00:08:14.060 --> 00:08:16.690
Secondly, you control
what happens to your data

00:08:16.690 --> 00:08:18.430
every time.

00:08:18.430 --> 00:08:20.740
Third, know where
Google stores your data,

00:08:20.740 --> 00:08:24.760
and rely on it being available
every single time you need it.

00:08:24.760 --> 00:08:27.910
Fourthly, depend on Google's
independently-verified security

00:08:27.910 --> 00:08:29.500
practices.

00:08:29.500 --> 00:08:32.080
And last but not least,
trust that we'll never

00:08:32.080 --> 00:08:35.260
give any government entity
backdoor access to your data

00:08:35.260 --> 00:08:38.210
or to any of our servers
that are storing your data.

00:08:38.210 --> 00:08:40.240
So I know we've covered
a lot of information

00:08:40.240 --> 00:08:43.150
in a short amount of time here
about how Google approaches

00:08:43.150 --> 00:08:45.160
and secures our
own infrastructure

00:08:45.160 --> 00:08:47.410
and what that means
for you, but don't just

00:08:47.410 --> 00:08:48.830
take our word for it.

00:08:48.830 --> 00:08:51.130
We believe in earning your
trust through transparency

00:08:51.130 --> 00:08:53.710
and not just with the technology
we've been talking about.

00:08:53.710 --> 00:08:55.251
So what I'm going
to do is, I'm going

00:08:55.251 --> 00:08:58.330
to pass the mic on and leave you
in the capable hands of James

00:08:58.330 --> 00:09:00.940
Snow, who's going to talk
to you about how Google

00:09:00.940 --> 00:09:04.240
Cloud approaches audits,
compliance, and what that means

00:09:04.240 --> 00:09:04.800
for you.

00:09:04.800 --> 00:09:05.800
Thank you for your time.

00:09:12.880 --> 00:09:15.130
JAMES SNOW: Thanks, Tanay,
and hello, everyone.

00:09:15.130 --> 00:09:15.990
I'm James Snow.

00:09:15.990 --> 00:09:18.480
I'm here to talk to you today
about security and compliance

00:09:18.480 --> 00:09:20.700
on the Google Cloud Platform.

00:09:20.700 --> 00:09:23.310
Now, to start, the first
question we often have

00:09:23.310 --> 00:09:26.190
is, what can Google
do with data once it's

00:09:26.190 --> 00:09:28.680
been put onto our
platform, otherwise known

00:09:28.680 --> 00:09:30.510
as scope of processing?

00:09:30.510 --> 00:09:32.880
The answer is very
straightforward.

00:09:32.880 --> 00:09:35.730
Google can only use
data on our platform

00:09:35.730 --> 00:09:38.650
to provide the service that you
as a customer have requested.

00:09:38.650 --> 00:09:41.970
So we can only use the
data per your instructions.

00:09:41.970 --> 00:09:44.230
And we can use it
for no other purpose.

00:09:44.230 --> 00:09:46.440
So this is outlined, not
only in our contracts,

00:09:46.440 --> 00:09:48.420
but it's also audited
by third parties,

00:09:48.420 --> 00:09:50.770
but we'll get to that
in just a moment.

00:09:50.770 --> 00:09:53.110
Next is intellectual property.

00:09:53.110 --> 00:09:54.810
Who owns the
intellectual property

00:09:54.810 --> 00:09:58.920
for data that is either uploaded
or generated on our platform?

00:09:58.920 --> 00:10:01.150
Again, a very simple answer.

00:10:01.150 --> 00:10:05.640
The customer has 100% of the IP
rights while Google has none.

00:10:05.640 --> 00:10:09.600
Last but not least, especially
relevant in regulated industry

00:10:09.600 --> 00:10:11.430
is around data portability.

00:10:11.430 --> 00:10:14.250
So if you move data onto
the Google Cloud Platform,

00:10:14.250 --> 00:10:15.730
can you easily move it off?

00:10:15.730 --> 00:10:17.070
And the answer is, yes.

00:10:17.070 --> 00:10:19.260
At any given time,
you can move your data

00:10:19.260 --> 00:10:21.900
on or off our platform
without requiring assistance

00:10:21.900 --> 00:10:25.140
from Google, with no
fines or fees, et cetera.

00:10:25.140 --> 00:10:28.230
And of course, this comes off
in industry-standard formats.

00:10:28.230 --> 00:10:32.010
You could think of Kubernetes,
our container orchestration

00:10:32.010 --> 00:10:35.370
platform, as the ultimate
embodiment of this philosophy,

00:10:35.370 --> 00:10:37.950
where you can move an
active workload from on-prem

00:10:37.950 --> 00:10:39.660
to Google's clouds
to other clouds

00:10:39.660 --> 00:10:44.940
dynamically in order to
meet uptime requirements.

00:10:44.940 --> 00:10:47.970
To pivot and talk about
managing regulatory risk-- so

00:10:47.970 --> 00:10:50.490
oftentimes, working with
regulated customers that

00:10:50.490 --> 00:10:53.590
could be anything from
national regulation to health

00:10:53.590 --> 00:10:56.160
care to government to finance.

00:10:56.160 --> 00:10:58.110
There are lots of
different regulatory risks

00:10:58.110 --> 00:11:00.490
that customers are exposed to.

00:11:00.490 --> 00:11:03.360
Now, if you were to
think like a regulator,

00:11:03.360 --> 00:11:05.520
the questions that
they're going to be asking

00:11:05.520 --> 00:11:09.030
is, how are you protecting
your regulated data?

00:11:09.030 --> 00:11:10.800
Who owns that data?

00:11:10.800 --> 00:11:13.830
Where is it located, and
how is it being used?

00:11:13.830 --> 00:11:18.180
Last but not least, if the
data is supposed to be deleted,

00:11:18.180 --> 00:11:21.580
how is that done
securely and efficiently?

00:11:21.580 --> 00:11:24.700
Now, at Google we have
a single cloud platform.

00:11:24.700 --> 00:11:27.900
So this actually makes it easier
for a lot of regulated industry

00:11:27.900 --> 00:11:31.170
because we operate a
baseline of security controls

00:11:31.170 --> 00:11:33.240
across our entire platform.

00:11:33.240 --> 00:11:37.110
So it's not one solution has one
level security or one product

00:11:37.110 --> 00:11:38.670
has one level security.

00:11:38.670 --> 00:11:41.580
It applies across
our entire platform.

00:11:41.580 --> 00:11:43.800
We already covered
ownership and usage.

00:11:43.800 --> 00:11:45.730
The customer owns the
data, and it can only

00:11:45.730 --> 00:11:47.790
be used per their instructions.

00:11:47.790 --> 00:11:50.850
Data location is
configurable per product

00:11:50.850 --> 00:11:53.220
that we have on the
Google Cloud Platform.

00:11:53.220 --> 00:11:55.560
At all times, it is
going to be transparent,

00:11:55.560 --> 00:11:58.110
and customers will know where
their data is at any given

00:11:58.110 --> 00:11:59.370
moment.

00:11:59.370 --> 00:12:04.680
Data deletion-- so Google is
what we call a data processor.

00:12:04.680 --> 00:12:06.960
The customer is the
data controller.

00:12:06.960 --> 00:12:09.240
If the data controller
gives Google an instruction

00:12:09.240 --> 00:12:11.430
to delete data, we as
the data controller

00:12:11.430 --> 00:12:16.460
will execute their instruction
faithfully and thoroughly.

00:12:16.460 --> 00:12:20.800
So lets talk about how
Google thinks about privacy

00:12:20.800 --> 00:12:22.690
and compliance and security.

00:12:22.690 --> 00:12:25.630
All of these topics
are interrelated.

00:12:25.630 --> 00:12:28.990
Now, in a traditional
IT system, this usually

00:12:28.990 --> 00:12:31.420
starts the other way around.

00:12:31.420 --> 00:12:34.340
They'll say, I need to process
or protect credit card data,

00:12:34.340 --> 00:12:37.820
so I need to build a
PCI DSS environment.

00:12:37.820 --> 00:12:39.680
Now, that's a starting
point for many,

00:12:39.680 --> 00:12:41.717
but for Google its
really reversed.

00:12:41.717 --> 00:12:43.300
We look at our entire
platform, and we

00:12:43.300 --> 00:12:45.130
say, what are the
strongest security

00:12:45.130 --> 00:12:46.780
controls that we
can put in place

00:12:46.780 --> 00:12:49.210
across our entire platform?

00:12:49.210 --> 00:12:51.040
Next, we'll look at
the privacy controls--

00:12:51.040 --> 00:12:53.620
the management of the data,
the access of the data--

00:12:53.620 --> 00:12:55.930
and being able to
monitor that effectively.

00:12:55.930 --> 00:12:58.990
Last but not least, we'll look
at the compliance frameworks

00:12:58.990 --> 00:13:00.730
that we can put on top of it.

00:13:00.730 --> 00:13:03.190
Because we have this
uniform security,

00:13:03.190 --> 00:13:05.080
we can actually start
looking at things

00:13:05.080 --> 00:13:07.900
like independent security
and compliance certifications

00:13:07.900 --> 00:13:12.250
as applying across our platform
and not for specific solutions

00:13:12.250 --> 00:13:14.570
or projects.

00:13:14.570 --> 00:13:17.350
So let's talk about our
independent third-party

00:13:17.350 --> 00:13:18.820
certifications.

00:13:18.820 --> 00:13:21.760
So please, the big difference
I want to impart here

00:13:21.760 --> 00:13:24.650
is that we operate a
single cloud environment.

00:13:24.650 --> 00:13:26.800
We have one global cloud.

00:13:26.800 --> 00:13:29.080
We do not have a German
cloud, or a health care cloud,

00:13:29.080 --> 00:13:31.900
or a financial services
cloud, or even a gov cloud.

00:13:31.900 --> 00:13:34.690
We operate a single
cloud infrastructure.

00:13:34.690 --> 00:13:36.640
And what that means is,
again, not just having

00:13:36.640 --> 00:13:40.510
a single baseline, but having
an audited baseline, again,

00:13:40.510 --> 00:13:44.110
against multiple standards
by multiple auditors.

00:13:44.110 --> 00:13:46.570
So lets talk through
a few of the biggies.

00:13:46.570 --> 00:13:48.670
So we have the ISO
certifications.

00:13:48.670 --> 00:13:51.370
This is an international
family of standards.

00:13:51.370 --> 00:13:56.050
Starting with ISO 27101 we
have 114 security controls

00:13:56.050 --> 00:13:57.760
looking at our platform.

00:13:57.760 --> 00:14:01.810
ISO 27017 is an evolution
with cloud-specific security

00:14:01.810 --> 00:14:03.310
controls.

00:14:03.310 --> 00:14:08.090
ISO 27018 is the first
cloud data privacy standard.

00:14:08.090 --> 00:14:09.980
So this is the
third-party audit that's

00:14:09.980 --> 00:14:13.690
attesting that your data is only
being used per your instruction

00:14:13.690 --> 00:14:15.580
and for no other purpose.

00:14:15.580 --> 00:14:18.160
If you're more the
report-reading type of security

00:14:18.160 --> 00:14:21.681
professional, we have our SO
1, SOC 2, and SOC 3 security

00:14:21.681 --> 00:14:22.180
reports.

00:14:22.180 --> 00:14:25.510
This is about 350 pages
of security goodness

00:14:25.510 --> 00:14:27.780
that you can review
and, of course,

00:14:27.780 --> 00:14:30.880
match to your controls
or your requirements.

00:14:30.880 --> 00:14:32.740
If you'd like to process
credit card data,

00:14:32.740 --> 00:14:34.750
we have PCI DSS certification.

00:14:34.750 --> 00:14:37.210
Of course, we have the
most recent certification.

00:14:37.210 --> 00:14:39.340
And we operate at the
highest merchant level,

00:14:39.340 --> 00:14:41.770
so we can meet your requirements
in terms of credit card

00:14:41.770 --> 00:14:42.970
processing.

00:14:42.970 --> 00:14:46.180
If you're a health care
professional, we have HIPAA.

00:14:46.180 --> 00:14:48.325
We actually support the
requirements under HIPAA,

00:14:48.325 --> 00:14:50.440
so we will enter into a BAA.

00:14:50.440 --> 00:14:53.830
We just recently attained
our high-trust certification

00:14:53.830 --> 00:14:56.590
focused on health care data.

00:14:56.590 --> 00:14:59.590
Last but not least, and this
is a large differentiation

00:14:59.590 --> 00:15:03.130
for our platform, is
FedRAMP authorization.

00:15:03.130 --> 00:15:05.800
So FedRAMP is the
security standard

00:15:05.800 --> 00:15:09.290
that's used for protecting data
at both the federal, state,

00:15:09.290 --> 00:15:10.720
and local levels.

00:15:10.720 --> 00:15:14.230
Now, on other platforms, they
have very specific government

00:15:14.230 --> 00:15:16.270
clouds that meet
these standards.

00:15:16.270 --> 00:15:18.820
At Google, we have
a global platform.

00:15:18.820 --> 00:15:21.670
So all of our data
centers, our services

00:15:21.670 --> 00:15:23.260
that are scoped under
FedRAMP, are all

00:15:23.260 --> 00:15:25.090
covered by these protections.

00:15:25.090 --> 00:15:27.700
And we make these same
services, of course,

00:15:27.700 --> 00:15:31.130
available to all customers,
not just government agencies.

00:15:31.130 --> 00:15:33.219
So if you're a bank
or in health care,

00:15:33.219 --> 00:15:35.260
your data is being protected
to the same standard

00:15:35.260 --> 00:15:36.676
that we use for
the US government.

00:15:36.676 --> 00:15:38.200
But it's not just FedRAMP.

00:15:38.200 --> 00:15:39.880
You have multiple audits--

00:15:39.880 --> 00:15:43.030
the FedRAMP audit, the ISO
audits, the SOC audits--

00:15:43.030 --> 00:15:45.310
multiple checks,
multiple times to ensure

00:15:45.310 --> 00:15:48.330
that your data is being
protected at the standards that

00:15:48.330 --> 00:15:49.930
have been promised.

00:15:49.930 --> 00:15:53.620
Now, these are the independent
third-party certifications.

00:15:53.620 --> 00:15:57.340
We also have a lot of
regulatory engagement.

00:15:57.340 --> 00:15:59.500
So being on that
single cloud platform

00:15:59.500 --> 00:16:02.410
is very important because
we have the exact same story

00:16:02.410 --> 00:16:04.210
that we can bring to
all of the regulators

00:16:04.210 --> 00:16:06.880
globally across
different industries.

00:16:06.880 --> 00:16:08.710
We're working with
national banks.

00:16:08.710 --> 00:16:11.300
We're working with health
care oversight authorities.

00:16:11.300 --> 00:16:13.720
We're working with privacy
authorities in Europe.

00:16:13.720 --> 00:16:16.390
We're working with them not
just to explain our platform

00:16:16.390 --> 00:16:20.080
and to quantify and
validate our contracts,

00:16:20.080 --> 00:16:22.600
but again, we want to
have a consistent picture

00:16:22.600 --> 00:16:26.600
for both compliance and security
across multiple geographies.

00:16:26.600 --> 00:16:29.500
So if you're a
multinational organization,

00:16:29.500 --> 00:16:31.480
you can have one
solution that's going

00:16:31.480 --> 00:16:33.250
to be able to protect
all of your users,

00:16:33.250 --> 00:16:36.580
rather than just having
something that is regional.

00:16:36.580 --> 00:16:37.810
All right.

00:16:37.810 --> 00:16:41.020
So speaking of something
that is a national level

00:16:41.020 --> 00:16:45.550
and topical of late, I'd
like to talk about the GDPR.

00:16:45.550 --> 00:16:50.260
The GDPR is the EU General
Data Protection Regulation.

00:16:50.260 --> 00:16:53.290
This affects and protects
the personally-identifiable

00:16:53.290 --> 00:16:55.810
information of
European nationals.

00:16:55.810 --> 00:16:58.210
Basically, it puts in
controls and restrictions

00:16:58.210 --> 00:17:00.340
on what can be done
with their data.

00:17:00.340 --> 00:17:02.470
And of course, it
gives them rights

00:17:02.470 --> 00:17:05.170
to be protected and notified
if there are things like data

00:17:05.170 --> 00:17:08.150
breaches or security incidents.

00:17:08.150 --> 00:17:10.240
Now, Google has been
working very hard,

00:17:10.240 --> 00:17:13.180
and we are compliant with this
regulation, both as a company

00:17:13.180 --> 00:17:17.619
and as a provider of services
via our enterprise cloud.

00:17:17.619 --> 00:17:19.660
Now, this matters
to most customers

00:17:19.660 --> 00:17:22.690
because oftentimes most
companies are actually

00:17:22.690 --> 00:17:25.240
interacting or collecting
this type of information.

00:17:25.240 --> 00:17:28.450
But again, you do not need
to make that differentiation.

00:17:28.450 --> 00:17:30.610
Because we operate
as one cloud, we're

00:17:30.610 --> 00:17:34.610
protecting all data to the same
level that the GDPR requires,

00:17:34.610 --> 00:17:36.520
not just a specific subset.

00:17:36.520 --> 00:17:40.240
So again, this is about
making things easier for you.

00:17:40.240 --> 00:17:44.110
The GDPR is evolutionary,
not revolutionary.

00:17:44.110 --> 00:17:46.420
For years, Google has been
compliant with the EU Data

00:17:46.420 --> 00:17:48.580
Protection Directive,
the regulation that

00:17:48.580 --> 00:17:51.070
came before the GDPR, and
we've been working for years

00:17:51.070 --> 00:17:55.190
to become complying with the
GDPR as a cloud provider.

00:17:55.190 --> 00:17:57.130
Now, when I say
it's evolutionary,

00:17:57.130 --> 00:18:00.100
it's evolutionary in a very
positive term for both data

00:18:00.100 --> 00:18:03.610
subjects and for
you as a customer.

00:18:03.610 --> 00:18:07.060
Under the old law, the Data
Protection Directive, only you,

00:18:07.060 --> 00:18:09.590
the customer, were
responsible for compliance.

00:18:09.590 --> 00:18:12.910
You were also responsible
for paying all these fines.

00:18:12.910 --> 00:18:15.820
Under the GDPR,
this is all changed.

00:18:15.820 --> 00:18:18.730
Under the GDPR,
security and compliance

00:18:18.730 --> 00:18:22.780
are a shared responsibility
between you and Google.

00:18:22.780 --> 00:18:24.900
For the areas that Google
is responsible for,

00:18:24.900 --> 00:18:28.360
we're responsible for directly
to the privacy authorities.

00:18:28.360 --> 00:18:30.444
If there is some sort
of incident or problem,

00:18:30.444 --> 00:18:32.110
it's Google who will
be held responsible

00:18:32.110 --> 00:18:34.180
and Google who'll
be paying the fine.

00:18:34.180 --> 00:18:37.600
We're here to partner with
you to help mitigate that risk

00:18:37.600 --> 00:18:41.461
and, of course, protect
your information.

00:18:41.461 --> 00:18:41.960
OK.

00:18:41.960 --> 00:18:43.552
I think that's all for today.

00:18:43.552 --> 00:18:45.260
I hope you learned a
lot today about what

00:18:45.260 --> 00:18:47.164
you need to know about
staying compliant.

00:18:47.164 --> 00:18:48.330
And thank you for your time.

00:18:55.084 --> 00:18:55.750
MIKE AIELLO: Hi.

00:18:55.750 --> 00:18:56.830
My name is Mike Aiello.

00:18:56.830 --> 00:18:59.740
I'm the director of product
management for Cloud Security.

00:18:59.740 --> 00:19:04.450
I'm here today to talk about
securing and sharing your data.

00:19:04.450 --> 00:19:07.690
So as a chief security officer,
chief information officer,

00:19:07.690 --> 00:19:11.230
chief risk officer you need
to manage risks differently

00:19:11.230 --> 00:19:12.649
when you move to Google Cloud.

00:19:12.649 --> 00:19:15.190
This is where most of your new
analysis and benefit of moving

00:19:15.190 --> 00:19:16.648
to the Cloud is
going to come from,

00:19:16.648 --> 00:19:18.970
but this drives changes
in enterprise culture,

00:19:18.970 --> 00:19:21.862
in audit, governance,
administrative control.

00:19:21.862 --> 00:19:23.320
And in order to
manage these risks,

00:19:23.320 --> 00:19:25.150
you need to think hard
about what the root

00:19:25.150 --> 00:19:27.070
cause of most of the risks are.

00:19:27.070 --> 00:19:29.230
And we find it comes
from three sources--

00:19:29.230 --> 00:19:32.950
poor data management practices,
unauthorized access control

00:19:32.950 --> 00:19:37.030
and access control setup, and
unencrypted data on the cloud.

00:19:37.030 --> 00:19:38.740
At Google, we're
proud to say that we

00:19:38.740 --> 00:19:41.650
have an array of solutions with
addressing these concerns that

00:19:41.650 --> 00:19:44.200
are part of the shared
responsibility between the user

00:19:44.200 --> 00:19:45.980
of Google Cloud and
Google Cloud itself.

00:19:45.980 --> 00:19:48.500
So let's go through them.

00:19:48.500 --> 00:19:51.070
First, we want to talk about
the exceptional opportunity

00:19:51.070 --> 00:19:53.677
for customer understanding,
product design, manufacturing,

00:19:53.677 --> 00:19:55.510
planning-- there's a
whole bunch of benefits

00:19:55.510 --> 00:19:57.640
that you get from
moving to Cloud.

00:19:57.640 --> 00:20:01.570
And the core of that is
the analytics workflow

00:20:01.570 --> 00:20:02.742
coming from Cloud.

00:20:02.742 --> 00:20:05.200
But this also means that you
have more data in more places.

00:20:05.200 --> 00:20:06.700
Previously, it was only on-prem.

00:20:06.700 --> 00:20:09.550
Now, it's on-prem and in-cloud
and across potentially multiple

00:20:09.550 --> 00:20:10.690
clouds.

00:20:10.690 --> 00:20:12.640
And a big part of the
reason you do this

00:20:12.640 --> 00:20:14.590
is because you want to
enable data sharing.

00:20:14.590 --> 00:20:17.380
The reality is in order to
get real value out of Cloud,

00:20:17.380 --> 00:20:19.000
you need to move
sensitive information

00:20:19.000 --> 00:20:21.280
or private information
to Cloud or anonymize

00:20:21.280 --> 00:20:23.680
versions of private
information in Cloud.

00:20:23.680 --> 00:20:25.684
One of the examples
that we have is we

00:20:25.684 --> 00:20:27.100
worked with a
health care customer

00:20:27.100 --> 00:20:30.002
who took more than two years
to simply identify and discover

00:20:30.002 --> 00:20:31.960
the private and sensitive
information that they

00:20:31.960 --> 00:20:33.640
had in their
enterprise before they

00:20:33.640 --> 00:20:36.004
began their adoption of Cloud.

00:20:36.004 --> 00:20:38.170
At Google, we've invested
deeply in machine learning

00:20:38.170 --> 00:20:41.320
and artificial intelligence to
create tools and products that

00:20:41.320 --> 00:20:43.600
make moving to Cloud
easier and make

00:20:43.600 --> 00:20:46.720
the data discovery and data
anonymization aspects easier.

00:20:46.720 --> 00:20:49.930
What you're seeing here is a
output of one of our products

00:20:49.930 --> 00:20:51.790
called the Data
Loss Prevention API,

00:20:51.790 --> 00:20:53.410
which has automatically
discovered

00:20:53.410 --> 00:20:57.520
sensitive information in a file
that was passed through the API

00:20:57.520 --> 00:21:00.712
and automatically in-place,
format-preserving redacted

00:21:00.712 --> 00:21:01.420
that information.

00:21:01.420 --> 00:21:04.000
So you can see that the phone
numbers have been redacted,

00:21:04.000 --> 00:21:05.882
except for the
first three digits,

00:21:05.882 --> 00:21:07.840
and email addresses and
social security numbers

00:21:07.840 --> 00:21:08.710
have been redacted.

00:21:08.710 --> 00:21:10.540
This makes it much
easier to tell

00:21:10.540 --> 00:21:13.510
the story of securely operating
analytics environments

00:21:13.510 --> 00:21:15.250
in the Cloud because
you can do so

00:21:15.250 --> 00:21:17.360
on anonymized and redacted data.

00:21:17.360 --> 00:21:19.240
We're also deeply
invested in making sure

00:21:19.240 --> 00:21:21.280
that you have
transparency into when

00:21:21.280 --> 00:21:23.510
a Googler accesses your data.

00:21:23.510 --> 00:21:26.120
So we've invested in a product
called Access Transparency,

00:21:26.120 --> 00:21:28.634
which you can enable, which
gives you a notification

00:21:28.634 --> 00:21:31.300
whenever a Googler accesses your
data-- for example, if you send

00:21:31.300 --> 00:21:34.300
in a support ticket asking for
help with a specific storage

00:21:34.300 --> 00:21:35.410
bucket that you have.

00:21:35.410 --> 00:21:38.410
So we're excited about having
tools and products that

00:21:38.410 --> 00:21:40.600
help you discover and
classify your information

00:21:40.600 --> 00:21:43.180
and, additionally, giving
you enhanced transparency

00:21:43.180 --> 00:21:46.300
when you keep sensitive
information in Google Cloud.

00:21:46.300 --> 00:21:48.064
Now let's talk about
access control--

00:21:48.064 --> 00:21:49.480
the idea that
people should access

00:21:49.480 --> 00:21:51.610
what they need for their
jobs and nothing else.

00:21:51.610 --> 00:21:54.610
It's a good fundamental
principle of security.

00:21:54.610 --> 00:21:58.654
At its core, it's defining who
can do what on which resource.

00:21:58.654 --> 00:22:00.070
So if you think
about who-- that's

00:22:00.070 --> 00:22:01.444
the identity of
the user-- can do

00:22:01.444 --> 00:22:03.470
what-- that's the action--
on which resource--

00:22:03.470 --> 00:22:06.140
this is the resource within
the Cloud, storage process, et

00:22:06.140 --> 00:22:07.000
cetera.

00:22:07.000 --> 00:22:08.560
We have two key
principles that we'd

00:22:08.560 --> 00:22:10.420
like to communicate about this.

00:22:10.420 --> 00:22:12.950
First is establish the most
granular access policies

00:22:12.950 --> 00:22:13.450
you can.

00:22:13.450 --> 00:22:15.100
It's the principle
of least privilege.

00:22:15.100 --> 00:22:17.100
And we provide an identity
and access management

00:22:17.100 --> 00:22:18.340
solution to do this.

00:22:18.340 --> 00:22:21.160
And then second is employ
multifactor authentication

00:22:21.160 --> 00:22:22.810
to protect against phishing.

00:22:22.810 --> 00:22:25.840
We found that this reduces
one of the most common attack

00:22:25.840 --> 00:22:30.790
factors that we see
in 2018 significantly.

00:22:30.790 --> 00:22:33.010
Finally, let's talk
about securing the bits--

00:22:33.010 --> 00:22:33.940
encryption.

00:22:33.940 --> 00:22:37.150
59% of companies today lack
an enterprise-wide encryption

00:22:37.150 --> 00:22:37.870
strategy.

00:22:37.870 --> 00:22:38.560
That's not good.

00:22:38.560 --> 00:22:40.510
There's many companies
out there that

00:22:40.510 --> 00:22:43.720
don't have a story they can
tell their board about data

00:22:43.720 --> 00:22:46.320
being encrypted by default
across the entire enterprise.

00:22:46.320 --> 00:22:48.820
You'll be happy to know that
as you move your data to Google

00:22:48.820 --> 00:22:50.350
Cloud we take care of that.

00:22:50.350 --> 00:22:52.600
We encrypt the
data by default. We

00:22:52.600 --> 00:22:54.940
encrypt it with the same
processes and procedures

00:22:54.940 --> 00:22:56.890
we use to encrypt
data across Google.

00:22:56.890 --> 00:22:59.290
And we've spent a long time
working on these processes

00:22:59.290 --> 00:23:01.610
and procedures.

00:23:01.610 --> 00:23:03.160
We have a core
principle that you

00:23:03.160 --> 00:23:06.040
should have control of the keys
that encrypt your data if you

00:23:06.040 --> 00:23:06.670
so choose.

00:23:06.670 --> 00:23:08.710
And we provide a range
of management options

00:23:08.710 --> 00:23:09.780
for those keys.

00:23:09.780 --> 00:23:11.530
So by default, like I
mentioned, your data

00:23:11.530 --> 00:23:14.770
is encrypted by REST with
keys that Google manages.

00:23:14.770 --> 00:23:17.560
At the other extreme is, you can
bring your own encryption keys.

00:23:17.560 --> 00:23:18.790
This gives you the
maximum control.

00:23:18.790 --> 00:23:20.165
You're providing
the key material

00:23:20.165 --> 00:23:21.220
which encrypts your data.

00:23:21.220 --> 00:23:23.844
And in the middle, we provide a
customer-managed encryption key

00:23:23.844 --> 00:23:26.620
option, where we generate
the keys for you,

00:23:26.620 --> 00:23:29.950
and you can control the
rotation and revocation periods

00:23:29.950 --> 00:23:31.460
around these keys.

00:23:31.460 --> 00:23:34.960
So in order to encrypt your
data and secure your data,

00:23:34.960 --> 00:23:36.954
you need a range of options.

00:23:36.954 --> 00:23:38.620
From a fundamental
perspective, the data

00:23:38.620 --> 00:23:40.570
is encrypted by
default at Google.

00:23:40.570 --> 00:23:43.360
And we can help you in
each of these choices

00:23:43.360 --> 00:23:46.840
that you have as you talk about
encryption with your customer

00:23:46.840 --> 00:23:49.220
base, with your board,
and with Google.

00:23:49.220 --> 00:23:52.510
And we see every permutation
of encryption key management

00:23:52.510 --> 00:23:53.800
that folks can think of.

00:23:53.800 --> 00:23:55.631
Folks have come to us
and talked about how

00:23:55.631 --> 00:23:57.130
they want encryption
to work, and we

00:23:57.130 --> 00:24:00.550
believe our product suite solves
the vast majority of these use

00:24:00.550 --> 00:24:01.090
cases.

00:24:01.090 --> 00:24:02.300
And if it doesn't
solve all of them,

00:24:02.300 --> 00:24:04.100
please come back and
talk to our team,

00:24:04.100 --> 00:24:06.920
and we will work to make
the products better.

00:24:06.920 --> 00:24:09.640
So with that, I wanted
to say thank you.

00:24:09.640 --> 00:24:11.380
And I wanted to hand
it over to Reena,

00:24:11.380 --> 00:24:14.338
who's going to talk about how
to work securely from anywhere.

00:24:20.992 --> 00:24:22.450
REENA NADKARNI:
Thank you, Michael.

00:24:22.450 --> 00:24:23.830
And hello, everyone.

00:24:23.830 --> 00:24:27.400
I am Reena Nadkarni, product
management lead on the G Suite

00:24:27.400 --> 00:24:28.660
team.

00:24:28.660 --> 00:24:31.600
G Suite is Google's
productivity suite.

00:24:31.600 --> 00:24:34.990
It includes all of the products
that you're familiar with,

00:24:34.990 --> 00:24:39.640
like Gmail, Calendar, Google
Drive, Docs, and Editors,

00:24:39.640 --> 00:24:43.300
with extra controls for
enterprise users and enterprise

00:24:43.300 --> 00:24:44.920
admins.

00:24:44.920 --> 00:24:48.190
My focus today is
primarily on security

00:24:48.190 --> 00:24:50.930
and how to make it simple.

00:24:50.930 --> 00:24:53.440
You can have all the
security tools in the world,

00:24:53.440 --> 00:24:57.350
but they're only useful if
you actually deploy them.

00:24:57.350 --> 00:25:01.510
We want to make security
simple, both for the IT admins,

00:25:01.510 --> 00:25:04.940
as well as for the
enterprise end users.

00:25:04.940 --> 00:25:09.160
So what can we do to make
security easy to use?

00:25:09.160 --> 00:25:12.070
Is it possible to
change the paradigm

00:25:12.070 --> 00:25:15.130
from the old world, where
employees are the greatest

00:25:15.130 --> 00:25:19.870
source of vulnerability,
the most mistrusted area,

00:25:19.870 --> 00:25:21.850
to switching it
around and making

00:25:21.850 --> 00:25:24.880
them the front line of defense?

00:25:24.880 --> 00:25:27.760
At Google, we have
a history of making

00:25:27.760 --> 00:25:30.440
difficult things simple to use.

00:25:30.440 --> 00:25:32.650
Think about quality
search results.

00:25:32.650 --> 00:25:34.480
That's something
that's very complex,

00:25:34.480 --> 00:25:37.930
has a lot of sophisticated
technology behind the scenes,

00:25:37.930 --> 00:25:41.260
but we've made it really
simple for our end users.

00:25:41.260 --> 00:25:44.200
Security is no exception.

00:25:44.200 --> 00:25:46.810
To many, this might
seem counterintuitive.

00:25:46.810 --> 00:25:51.550
Security implies levers, dials,
lots of rules, and engines.

00:25:51.550 --> 00:25:54.670
Easy implies exactly
the opposite.

00:25:54.670 --> 00:25:58.000
Making security easy
might scare some people,

00:25:58.000 --> 00:26:00.130
but I would argue
that the failure

00:26:00.130 --> 00:26:04.450
to not achieve this goal is
far worse because then security

00:26:04.450 --> 00:26:08.620
gets in your way and people are
not really using the security

00:26:08.620 --> 00:26:11.510
tools we make available.

00:26:11.510 --> 00:26:14.800
So with great urgency, we're
driving towards the world

00:26:14.800 --> 00:26:17.050
that solves this problem.

00:26:17.050 --> 00:26:19.900
Let's think about the way
in which many people still

00:26:19.900 --> 00:26:21.220
work today--

00:26:21.220 --> 00:26:24.230
passwords being managed
across different systems.

00:26:24.230 --> 00:26:27.550
Some systems require passwords
that have 12 characters.

00:26:27.550 --> 00:26:30.220
Others want a mix of upper
and lower characters.

00:26:30.220 --> 00:26:32.590
Some require special characters.

00:26:32.590 --> 00:26:35.139
And many times people
simply pick passwords,

00:26:35.139 --> 00:26:37.180
write them on a Post-it,
stick them in their cube

00:26:37.180 --> 00:26:38.830
or on their screen.

00:26:38.830 --> 00:26:41.470
Many people are still
using thumb drives for data

00:26:41.470 --> 00:26:43.150
portability.

00:26:43.150 --> 00:26:45.370
And countless hours
are being spent

00:26:45.370 --> 00:26:47.260
in attending trainings
and developing

00:26:47.260 --> 00:26:49.320
trainings around compliance--

00:26:49.320 --> 00:26:51.220
the trainings that
I like to call

00:26:51.220 --> 00:26:54.850
"thou shall not do x, y, or z."

00:26:54.850 --> 00:26:58.690
So let's start to think about
how we get control over all

00:26:58.690 --> 00:27:02.830
these issues and how we can
solve these problems by knowing

00:27:02.830 --> 00:27:03.400
the risks.

00:27:06.970 --> 00:27:09.980
To understand the landscape
a little bit better,

00:27:09.980 --> 00:27:11.830
let's look at what's
been happening

00:27:11.830 --> 00:27:15.610
in the world of data breaches
and the world of ransomware.

00:27:15.610 --> 00:27:22.600
There has been a 15x increase in
ransomware losses between 2015

00:27:22.600 --> 00:27:26.170
to 2017, the last
couple of years.

00:27:26.170 --> 00:27:28.750
Employees are more
vulnerable than ever.

00:27:28.750 --> 00:27:31.390
90% of all reported
breaches were

00:27:31.390 --> 00:27:35.500
caused by unintentional
employee negligence.

00:27:35.500 --> 00:27:39.550
These bad actors prey on
your vulnerable employees.

00:27:39.550 --> 00:27:42.430
Data breaches, when
compounded over time,

00:27:42.430 --> 00:27:45.520
are estimated to have
a collective cost

00:27:45.520 --> 00:27:50.530
of $2.1 trillion by 2019.

00:27:50.530 --> 00:27:52.510
So it's really
easy to understand

00:27:52.510 --> 00:27:56.290
why we need solutions that
not only help the IT admins

00:27:56.290 --> 00:28:00.170
but also empower the
enterprise end users.

00:28:00.170 --> 00:28:05.720
So now let's see how Cloud
Native Solution can help.

00:28:05.720 --> 00:28:08.840
In security, scale matters.

00:28:08.840 --> 00:28:13.220
It's almost like we constantly
see these new attacks and flaws

00:28:13.220 --> 00:28:16.890
from using old methods
and outdated patches.

00:28:16.890 --> 00:28:20.010
This is because we are
running a network that carries

00:28:20.010 --> 00:28:22.590
1/4 of the internet traffic.

00:28:22.590 --> 00:28:26.370
We stop more than anyone else
and are uniquely equipped

00:28:26.370 --> 00:28:29.110
to address them all.

00:28:29.110 --> 00:28:34.960
Did you know that every minute
Gmail stops 10 million spam

00:28:34.960 --> 00:28:38.650
and malicious emails from
reaching our end users?

00:28:38.650 --> 00:28:42.850
We've also recently added
advanced spoofing protections

00:28:42.850 --> 00:28:43.960
in Gmail.

00:28:43.960 --> 00:28:46.330
We've added Gmail
Confidential, which

00:28:46.330 --> 00:28:49.070
allows you to set
message expiration,

00:28:49.070 --> 00:28:52.960
revoke sent messages, and also
limit attachment downloads

00:28:52.960 --> 00:28:57.940
and forwarding to keep your
confidential data safe.

00:28:57.940 --> 00:29:00.340
We talked a little
bit about scale.

00:29:00.340 --> 00:29:03.010
The scale that we have
allows us to apply

00:29:03.010 --> 00:29:07.690
advanced AI and ML techniques
to the domain of security.

00:29:07.690 --> 00:29:10.120
As you know, machine
learning models

00:29:10.120 --> 00:29:12.520
scale better based
on the data that you

00:29:12.520 --> 00:29:15.460
have to train these models on.

00:29:15.460 --> 00:29:17.620
Our machine learning
models train

00:29:17.620 --> 00:29:21.730
on data generated from seven
products that have over

00:29:21.730 --> 00:29:25.060
a billion monthly active users.

00:29:25.060 --> 00:29:29.290
Secondly, when you think about
attacks, like DDoS attacks,

00:29:29.290 --> 00:29:33.160
you want to have a
high-bandwidth network.

00:29:33.160 --> 00:29:35.290
We have the network
that allows you

00:29:35.290 --> 00:29:39.850
to watch 500,000 YouTube
videos in high definition

00:29:39.850 --> 00:29:42.020
simultaneously.

00:29:42.020 --> 00:29:44.290
And then think about
device protections.

00:29:44.290 --> 00:29:47.830
We protect 3 billion
devices from URLs

00:29:47.830 --> 00:29:50.950
with malicious
content every day.

00:29:50.950 --> 00:29:53.830
And with Google, this
scale and security

00:29:53.830 --> 00:29:57.790
is now available to protect
you, your corporation,

00:29:57.790 --> 00:30:00.650
and your employees
and their data.

00:30:00.650 --> 00:30:03.310
So let's talk about
some specific features

00:30:03.310 --> 00:30:06.190
that we have to be able to
make your employees less

00:30:06.190 --> 00:30:08.740
vulnerable and more powerful.

00:30:08.740 --> 00:30:12.030
The first, I want to
start with a statistic--

00:30:12.030 --> 00:30:13.150
zero.

00:30:13.150 --> 00:30:16.450
Zero is the number
of account hijackings

00:30:16.450 --> 00:30:21.040
we have seen detected or
reported from our customers

00:30:21.040 --> 00:30:24.140
ever since they have
deployed security keys.

00:30:24.140 --> 00:30:26.440
So this is a very simple
and very important

00:30:26.440 --> 00:30:31.240
security protection that you
can use for your organizations.

00:30:31.240 --> 00:30:34.570
Second, G Suite Data
Loss Prevention--

00:30:34.570 --> 00:30:37.560
both in Gmail and
in Google Drive,

00:30:37.560 --> 00:30:40.870
at scale we can
stop important data

00:30:40.870 --> 00:30:44.500
from being exfiltrated
outside your organizations.

00:30:44.500 --> 00:30:48.670
And we make it really simple
for you to set up these rules--

00:30:48.670 --> 00:30:50.050
the DLP rules.

00:30:50.050 --> 00:30:53.380
We have over 50
predefined content types

00:30:53.380 --> 00:30:56.680
that cover confidential
data across the world

00:30:56.680 --> 00:31:00.820
so that you can create very
easily those advanced DLP

00:31:00.820 --> 00:31:01.710
rules.

00:31:01.710 --> 00:31:04.870
But we also allow you
to create custom rules

00:31:04.870 --> 00:31:06.830
so that you can protect data.

00:31:06.830 --> 00:31:09.430
Let's say that you have a
confidential project name,

00:31:09.430 --> 00:31:12.160
and you want to make certain
that the data related

00:31:12.160 --> 00:31:16.840
to this project never goes
outside of your organization.

00:31:16.840 --> 00:31:20.170
You can set up a DLP
rule to protect that.

00:31:20.170 --> 00:31:22.580
And you can take a variety
of different actions.

00:31:22.580 --> 00:31:26.470
For example, you can quarantine
that email from going out,

00:31:26.470 --> 00:31:29.890
you can warn the user,
or you can do both.

00:31:29.890 --> 00:31:33.585
And lastly, we now have
Security Center for G Suite.

00:31:33.585 --> 00:31:35.750
What Security Center
for G Suite does,

00:31:35.750 --> 00:31:38.980
is that first of all, it
has a set of dashboards

00:31:38.980 --> 00:31:43.000
that consolidates data from
across the organization--

00:31:43.000 --> 00:31:47.200
things like log-in audit,
mobile device management alerts,

00:31:47.200 --> 00:31:51.650
as well as suspicious drive
activity-- all in one place.

00:31:51.650 --> 00:31:54.070
So as an admin, you
can come in and get

00:31:54.070 --> 00:31:58.880
a single view of the security
posture of your organization.

00:31:58.880 --> 00:32:01.960
Second, we have consolidated
all the security

00:32:01.960 --> 00:32:05.650
settings for your
organization in one place.

00:32:05.650 --> 00:32:08.320
And we show you
Google-recommended best

00:32:08.320 --> 00:32:12.010
practices of what those settings
should be to best protect

00:32:12.010 --> 00:32:13.670
your organization.

00:32:13.670 --> 00:32:15.100
So what you really
see is a health

00:32:15.100 --> 00:32:18.160
check of your organization's
security settings,

00:32:18.160 --> 00:32:21.490
all the settings in one
place, what Google recommends

00:32:21.490 --> 00:32:25.450
are the best practices, as
well as what settings you have

00:32:25.450 --> 00:32:28.840
and where you're deviating
from our best practices.

00:32:28.840 --> 00:32:31.900
This has been really
useful for our IT admins

00:32:31.900 --> 00:32:35.150
to get at a glance in one view.

00:32:35.150 --> 00:32:38.650
And then finally, we have
created an investigation

00:32:38.650 --> 00:32:42.610
tool that allows you to
very quickly take actions.

00:32:42.610 --> 00:32:45.730
This is because when a
malicious email enters

00:32:45.730 --> 00:32:48.220
into your organization,
the time that you

00:32:48.220 --> 00:32:50.890
have to protect
your data requires

00:32:50.890 --> 00:32:52.760
you to act very quickly.

00:32:52.760 --> 00:32:57.310
So what we do is we allow the
admins to very quickly navigate

00:32:57.310 --> 00:33:00.820
across different core pieces
of data, from mobile logins

00:33:00.820 --> 00:33:02.770
into suspicious
drive activities,

00:33:02.770 --> 00:33:05.440
and narrow down the
data that you're seeing

00:33:05.440 --> 00:33:07.450
and very quickly take actions--

00:33:07.450 --> 00:33:10.120
bulk actions, like
deleting emails.

00:33:10.120 --> 00:33:12.970
Now, clearly this is a
very powerful capability,

00:33:12.970 --> 00:33:15.010
and so we want to
make sure that it's

00:33:15.010 --> 00:33:18.010
available only to the
super admins with very

00:33:18.010 --> 00:33:20.680
delegated and privileged access.

00:33:20.680 --> 00:33:23.060
So we talked about three things.

00:33:23.060 --> 00:33:26.590
First, how you can
protect your organization

00:33:26.590 --> 00:33:28.750
by deploying security keys.

00:33:28.750 --> 00:33:31.750
Second, how you can
proactively set up

00:33:31.750 --> 00:33:35.530
DLP rules to better protect
your organization's data.

00:33:35.530 --> 00:33:38.860
And finally, how G Suite
Security Center helps

00:33:38.860 --> 00:33:43.450
your organization get a one-stop
view of the security posture

00:33:43.450 --> 00:33:47.670
and very quickly act upon
malicious incidences.

00:33:47.670 --> 00:33:51.160
With that, I want to
thank you for watching.

00:33:51.160 --> 00:33:53.290
I really hope you
enjoyed this talk.

00:33:53.290 --> 00:33:56.560
And we'll end with some insights
from one of our customers,

00:33:56.560 --> 00:33:58.600
the City of Los Angeles.

00:33:58.600 --> 00:34:01.990
I'd like to now hand it
over to Ted Ross, who is

00:34:01.990 --> 00:34:04.630
their general manager and CEO.

00:34:04.630 --> 00:34:07.505
Thank you.

00:34:07.505 --> 00:34:10.858
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:34:12.631 --> 00:34:14.130
TED ROSS: My name
is Ted Ross, and I

00:34:14.130 --> 00:34:17.489
am chief information officer
at the City of Los Angeles.

00:34:17.489 --> 00:34:19.710
As the IT department for
the second largest city

00:34:19.710 --> 00:34:22.580
in the United States, we
have plenty of challenges.

00:34:22.580 --> 00:34:25.199
We're relied upon by many
other city departments.

00:34:25.199 --> 00:34:26.969
That includes LA
Police Department,

00:34:26.969 --> 00:34:30.810
libraries, recreation and parks,
building and safety, planning,

00:34:30.810 --> 00:34:32.820
and many others.

00:34:32.820 --> 00:34:34.739
In the beginning, people
thought of the cloud

00:34:34.739 --> 00:34:37.650
as being something a little
more risky because your data is

00:34:37.650 --> 00:34:40.320
no longer on premise,
something that you can touch,

00:34:40.320 --> 00:34:42.210
but we're really
way past that point.

00:34:42.210 --> 00:34:44.310
Security in Cloud is
exponentially better

00:34:44.310 --> 00:34:46.770
than what most organizations
can provide on premise.

00:34:46.770 --> 00:34:48.929
In 2018, we moved
our financial system

00:34:48.929 --> 00:34:51.090
into the cloud, which
means hundreds of thousands

00:34:51.090 --> 00:34:55.020
of transactions every year
are running out of the cloud.

00:34:55.020 --> 00:34:56.969
By moving data up
into the cloud,

00:34:56.969 --> 00:35:00.270
not only do we get a low-cost,
pay-as-you-go infrastructure

00:35:00.270 --> 00:35:03.699
that's secure and resilient,
but we also get access to great

00:35:03.699 --> 00:35:06.240
machine learning and artificial
intelligence tools that allow

00:35:06.240 --> 00:35:08.730
us to gain much more
insight into our operations,

00:35:08.730 --> 00:35:11.370
how we run our city, the
decisions that we make,

00:35:11.370 --> 00:35:14.040
and we can do so with powerful
tools that we wouldn't be able

00:35:14.040 --> 00:35:17.450
to access within
our data center.

00:35:17.450 --> 00:35:21.010
In 2017, we had a
very unique situation.

00:35:21.010 --> 00:35:23.420
Two multi-alarm fires
occurred in Los Angeles

00:35:23.420 --> 00:35:24.650
at the same time.

00:35:24.650 --> 00:35:28.280
We had to get communications out
to over 150,000 residents who

00:35:28.280 --> 00:35:29.390
had to be evacuated.

00:35:29.390 --> 00:35:31.700
So while we did it with
all the traditional means

00:35:31.700 --> 00:35:34.670
of news stories and
radio reports, et cetera,

00:35:34.670 --> 00:35:36.840
we were missing a
digital solution.

00:35:36.840 --> 00:35:39.800
We leveraged Google Maps to
very quickly put together

00:35:39.800 --> 00:35:42.720
layers, very simple layers,
to communicate where

00:35:42.720 --> 00:35:45.440
the fire evacuations were.

00:35:45.440 --> 00:35:47.690
The use of Google Maps
for fire evacuation

00:35:47.690 --> 00:35:49.550
was so effective
that we actually

00:35:49.550 --> 00:35:53.780
had over three million
views within 36 hours.

00:35:53.780 --> 00:35:57.140
And honestly, you will
not find such a high level

00:35:57.140 --> 00:35:59.910
of consumption for almost
anything government does.

00:35:59.910 --> 00:36:02.070
We feel that cloud
infrastructure is absolutely

00:36:02.070 --> 00:36:03.020
the way of the future.

00:36:03.020 --> 00:36:06.400
It allows us to deliver great
technology that's robust,

00:36:06.400 --> 00:36:11.080
it's resilient to disasters,
and it scales at internet scale.

00:36:11.080 --> 00:36:14.050
We find G Suite tools to be
an extremely effective way

00:36:14.050 --> 00:36:16.100
of empowering our employees.

00:36:16.100 --> 00:36:17.860
Here's a service and
here's a tool that

00:36:17.860 --> 00:36:19.867
makes our employees
much more productive,

00:36:19.867 --> 00:36:21.700
whether they're sharing
documents or sharing

00:36:21.700 --> 00:36:23.770
